The wind on the Isle of Solitude did not merely blow; it screamed. It tore at the stone walls of the lighthouse, rattling the brass fittings and shaking the dust from the high shelves. For Elias, the sound was a lullaby, a constant reminder that he was alone, and that the world beyond the glass was a chaotic, indifferent place. He had been the keeper for twenty years, a period so long that the rhythm of the beam had become his second heartbeat.

One Tuesday, when the sky was the color of bruised iron, the mail boat arrived. It was a small, battered vessel that looked like it might capsize in a breeze half the strength of the current. The mailman, a young man with a face weathered by salt spray, handed Elias a single envelope. It was thick, cream-colored, and smelled faintly of lavender and old paper.

Elias wiped his hands on his oil-stained trousers and broke the seal. The letter was written in a delicate, looping script that seemed to dance on the page.

*Dear Mr. Thorne,*

*My name is Elara. I am a cartographer in London. I have never met you, nor have I ever seen the Isle of Sol
